<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Teck</h1><p class="st-meta">1187 CE – 1439 CE (approx.) · capital Kirchheim unter Teck</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swabian duchy of a Zähringen branch named after Teck castle near Kirchheim, a ducal title without a duchy that later passed to the British royal family's ancestors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Adalbert, a younger son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-duke-of-zahringen">Conrad of Zähringen</a>, inherited lands around the castle of Teck on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a> and called himself Duke of Teck from 1187. The ducal title came from his family's rank rather than any ancient duchy, and the lands were modest, centred on Kirchheim and Owen.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled a small cluster of castles, towns and farmland and often served greater lords, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The family split into lines and, under financial pressure, sold its holdings piece by piece. Most of the territory, including Kirchheim, was sold to the Counts of Württemberg in 1381 to 1386.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last duke, Ludwig, Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, died in 1439. Württemberg later took the ducal title, and in 1871 it was revived for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-duke-of-teck">Francis of Teck</a>, father of Queen Mary of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. Teck castle survives in part.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-teck.jpg" alt="Duchy of Teck" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Merkur-kun · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Teck_Vorderansicht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1187 CE – 1439 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kirchheim unter Teck</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adalbert I of Teck, Ludwig of Teck</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Teck</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1313&amp;focus=duchy-of-teck" aria-label="Duchy of Teck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 480 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Teck lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5219 736L5229 694M5219 736L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1313&amp;focus=duchy-of-teck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
